message grabbed</div><div> &gt;&gt; from the writeup:</div><div> &gt;&gt=
; </div><div> &gt;&gt; =E2=80=9CIt does not modify TLS directly, but rat=
her using existing mechanisms and code</div><div> &gt;&gt; point registr=
ies. It does not define any specific hybrid key exchanges.=E2=80=9D</div=
><div> &gt;&gt; </div><div> &gt;&gt; This statement may be revisited bas=
ed on the outcome of the next item.</div><div> &gt;&gt; </div><div> &gt;=
&gt; # Relaxing a MUST in the base TLS spec?</div><div> &gt;&gt; </div><=
div> &gt;&gt; CURRENT:</div><div> &gt;&gt;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; [TLS13] req=
uires that ``The key_exchange values for each</div><div> &gt;&gt;&nbsp;&=
nbsp;&nbsp; KeyShareEntry MUST be generated independently.'' In the cont=
ext of</div><div> &gt;&gt;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; this document, since the sa=
me algorithm may appear in multiple named</div><div> &gt;&gt;&nbsp;&nbsp=
;&nbsp; groups, this document relaxes the above requirement to allow the=
 same</div><div> &gt;&gt;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; key_exchange value for the s=
ame algorithm to be reused in multiple</div><div> &gt;&gt;&nbsp;&nbsp;&n=
bsp; KeyShareEntry records sent in within the same ClientHello.</div><di=
v> &gt;&gt; </div><div> &gt;&gt; Isn=E2=80=99t this modifying aspects of=
 the base TLS? How to reconcile this with the</div><div> &gt;&gt; claim =
in the previous point?</div><div> &gt;&gt; </div><div> &gt;&gt; From a t=
echnical perspective I'd like to revisit this point. My memory is that t=
he</div><div> &gt;&gt; plausible KEMs actually have fast key generation,=
 as does EC. Why not just</div><div> &gt;&gt; keep the requirement as-is=
?</div><div> &gt; </div><div> &gt; </div><div> &gt; This seems like a su=
fficiently significant change (on text that has been present in the draf=
t since 2020) that I=E2=80=99ll need guidance from the working group / c=
hairs on how to proceed.</div><div> &gt; </div><div> &gt; Do any impleme=
ntations currently do that?&nbsp; My quick check of Chrome using Wiresha=
rk seems to show different EC points used in the two transmitted keyshar=
es (MLKEM768X25519 versus X25519), but that=E2=80=99s not an exhaustive =
check.</div><div> &gt; </div><div> &gt; Douglas</div><div> &gt; </div><d=
